  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure at the surface will remain in control through the \nearly part of the weekend with strong upper level ridging in place \nover the eastern third of the CONUS. This is translating to 850mb \ntemperatures in the upper single digits Celsius and surface \ntemperatures 10-15F above normal for Thursday through Saturday. \nFurther west into the plains region, low pressure system ejecting \nout of the intermountain west will be forced northeastward given the \naforementioned upper level ridge, keeping the pattern quiet across \nthe southern Great Lakes. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nUpper level ridge will finally break down through the weekend thanks \nto a trough aloft swinging southeastward out of western Canada. This \nwill work with a trough in the southern stream kicking out of the \nGreat Basin to force out the ridge over the eastern CONUS, leading \nto a wholesale pattern and sensible weather change for the region. \nShower activity with a leading cold front will likely be scattered \nin nature, and quick to move through, resulting in very modest QPF. \nTemperatures in the wake of the front will drop sharply and high \ntemperatures early next week will be in the upper 30s/lower 40s.",
